            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, this house is relatively air tight and has very little mechanical ventilation - one bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer. If it was being built today, there would be a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit installed &amp; I talk further about this is the Ventilation section of this report.



During your renovations &amp; mechanical equipment upgrades, this house will become tighter in the following locations:



** The foundation header space will be sealed



** The holes in the kitchen walls will be sealed



** The attic access panel can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as "Draft Attack"



** The walls will become more air tight as additional exterior wall insulation &amp; a full air barrier wrap is installed



** The chimney will be eliminated when you upgrade to a high efficiency, direct-vent furnace &amp; hot water system.

    The chimney access up through the basement ceiling through to the attic has alot of leakage



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 59 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. If you complete most of the air sealing efforts in the list above, it is highly likely the reduction in air leakage will become 20% or higher, doubling the energy savings &amp; increasing the rebate to $750.



NOTE: as mentioned above, this house is already very air tight, so a further reduction in air leakage increases the need for an HRV.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and have 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene insulation on the exterior. We discussed your interest to update the exterior siding of this house and this is a great time to add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding another R10 to the exterior of the walls. You mentioned that you may even want to add R20 to maximize the rebate - so I modeled this as well !



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 171 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



If you were to add R20, you could potentially save $ 254 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The basement walls are bare concrete with the top 30% above grade and fully exposed to the cold outside temperatures. The header space above the concrete has R12 insulation. We talked about adding R24 to the interior of the concrete walls &amp; the header space should also be increased by a minimum R20 (can be added to interior and/or exterior as part of the exterior wall insulation &amp; siding upgrade.



The R24 for the concrete walls can be achieved with 2" (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene against the concrete with R14 batt insulation within a 2x4 perimeter wall. 



Savings: By adding a minimum R24 to the concrete walls &amp; a minimum R20 to the header space above, you could potentially save $ 1,743 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 71.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,750 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house is generally air tight, except for some air leaks (outlined elsewhere in this report) that will be eliminated as you complete your renovations and upgrade the furnace &amp; hot water. Many tighter houses from the 70's would not have had an HRV. However, they would try to manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by incorporating a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



If this house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. As your basement is completely undeveloped, the opportunity to install an HRV is still quite good !



As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 70% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



Savings:

By installing a 70% efficient ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will actually increase the ventilation of this house (which is a good thing) and there is as estimated additional $ 105 per year in energy costs, and an increase to your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ (which is about the same as the savings from air sealing if you achieve a 20% reduction). There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre gas-fired hot water tank, co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. You may want to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient gas furnace. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 615 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500   from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12,250 in rebates (perhaps $17,500)  from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation ($8,000 if you add R20)

     - $2,750 for the basement insulation

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $   500 for the air leakage reduction ($750 if you achieve a 20% reduction)

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with the main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate and the basement insulation, however these rebates would only be 50% of what is listed above (or $2,875). So ............. in a way, there is a potential to receive up to $9,375 if you do choose to add a minimum R10 of main floor wall insulation, or up to $14,375 if you wanted to add R20 to the main floor walls !!



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR washing machine - $200

     - ESTAR refrigerator - $200

     - ESTAR on-demand hot water heater - $1,250

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the bathroom fan, as the kitchen range hood fan does not vent outside &amp; the clothes dryer was not connected), indicated a pressure drop of 0.2 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; hot water tank co-vent up the same chimney (there is no dedicated outside combustion air supply in the mechanical room) so these appliances are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see some older smoke detectors but did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - There is one bathroom exhaust fan



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $0.62 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



